==Aftermath==
The match has some cultural significance in both countries.

For Brazil, they went on to win the match for third place, after losing to the Italians in the semi-finals. The 3rd place occupation by Brazil was seen to be Brazil's greatest football achievement by the time, and helped boosting football's popularity in the country. It was the 1938 edition that Brazil started to rise to become a football superpower, although it took them [[1958 FIFA World Cup|20 years to finally win their first World Cup]], against the [[Sweden men's national football team|hosts]].

It was Poland's first and only World Cup match in the [[Interwar period]]. Poland was later invaded by [[Nazi Germany]] and [[Soviet Union]] a year later, and Poland suffered significant destruction of its sporting developments, including football. Following the end of [[World War II]], the [[Polish People's Republic]] was established under [[Joseph Stalin|Stalin]]'s control. Thus, Polish football did not make any major impact until their return in [[1974 FIFA World Cup|1974]], where the Poles surprised the whole tournament by achieving third place, by taking vengeance on the Brazilians in their final match. Polish golden generation by then, was born.

Brazil and Poland would meet each other three more times in the FIFA World Cup (1–0 for Poland in 1974, 3–1 to Brazil in 1978 and 4–0 for Brazil in 1986).

